{"kind":"assert","text":"Blocked on the inbound half reaching main, discovered on starting rather than predicted. Recording it because it will outlive this session and because the promise above should not read as work in flight.\n\nThe request says it rests on the inbound half being in place, and I read that as a sequencing note rather than a hard dependency. It is a hard dependency: `git ls-tree -r --name-only main | grep -c internal/connector` returns 0. The whole `internal/connector/github` package — Clause, Fetch, Observation, the charter check — exists only on request/github-connector-observe. A worktree cut from main has nothing to build the outbound half on.\n\nI made and removed such a worktree rather than leave an empty branch lying around.\n\nThe two ways forward both have a cost worth stating. Branching from the inbound head a36ec7fa would let me start now, but that head is under review and codex has already requested changes on it twice; every change to it moves the base under the outbound work, and the second review would be judging a diff whose parent is itself unreviewed. Waiting for the merge costs time and nothing else. I am waiting.\n\nSo the chain is: codex's verdict on a36ec7fa, then the merge, then this. Nothing else is needed from anyone — the outbound charter is a separate gate that only bites when the connector first writes to GitHub, and by then Hugh will have had the built shape to write a charter against."}